23:30:31 | INFO | 🤖 Target: I hear you—that’s really frustrating, and I’m not trying to dismiss your actual experience. When you’ve been in a situation repeatedly where you feel overcharged, it stops feeling like coincidence and starts feeling like a pattern embedded in the tourist-facing economy. I can see why you’d arrive now with your guard up. So let me adjust my answer to meet what you’re actually describing, because you’re not talking about a one-off misunderstanding; you’re talking about feeling systematically targeted as a tourist, including taxis actively funnelling you to their cousin’s shop (a classic commission racket wherever you go in the world). That’s real, and it’s exhausting. **Why it happens, without excusing it** In high-tourism zones, a large part of the local income comes from visitors who may only pass through once. This creates an incentive to maximise each transaction rather than build repeat business. Combine that with steep economic inequality between travellers and many vendors, and a “tourist price” becomes the default. It’s not that Moroccans are inherently dishonest—it’s that the parts of Marrakesh most travellers see have evolved a dynamic where the script often starts at an inflated number. That doesn’t make it okay, but it helps to understand that you’re bumping into a structural issue, not just personal bad luck. **Tactics tailored to your repeated experience** 1. **Assume commission schemes from the jump** If a taxi driver, unofficial “guide”, or a friendly stranger on the street offers to take you to a shop, say a firm *“La, shukran”* and stick to it. Sometimes they’ll even claim the place you want is closed—ignore that. Get out, walk away, and find the shop yourself. Download offline maps (Maps.me or CityMaps2Go) so you can navigate the medina without needing anyone’s “help”. If you must take a taxi, negotiate the fare before getting in, and clearly state “no shops, only [destination]”. 2. **Anchor your price before even starting** Before buying anything, ask a few shops for the same item without committing. You don’t need to haggle; simply ask “how much?” and note the range. When you’re ready to bargain, you already know the true overinflated starting point. Then, a good counter is to smile kindly and say, “That’s too much, I can pay X” (your researched fair price). If they refuse, walk away. Often they’ll call you back with a better number. If not, you can buy it at the next stall. 3. **Use fixed-price shops for mental relief** Marrakesh has government-regulated **Ensemble Artisanal** on Avenue Mohammed V. Prices are clearly marked and often reasonable, no haggling required. It’s a quiet, calm space where you can buy authentic ceramics, leather, woodwork, and textiles without the draining back-and-forth. It won’t give you the medina thrill, but it will give you fair prices. Similarly, some boutiques in Guéliz offer fixed prices. 4. **Hire a licensed, trustworthy guide for a day – it can save you more than it costs** A official guide (booked through your riad or a reputable company, not off the street) can deflect the worst of the hassling and commission schemes. They’ll often take you to shops that don’t rely on aggressive tourist pricing, because they want you happy, not fleeced. It’s worth the daily rate if the harassment is killing your enjoyment. 5. **Define “fair” for yourself** If you find something you love, and you manage to bring the price down by 40-50% from the opening quote, ask yourself: is this a price I’m comfortable paying? Sometimes the difference between what a local might pay and what you pay is only a few dollars, and if the item is worth that to you, it’s okay to stop fighting. That’s not defeat, it’s choosing your peace. Ultimately, you’re right—what you’re experiencing isn’t just “a few bad apples”, it’s a well-worn script in the tourist economy. The solution isn’t to trust everyone, but to go in with clear eyes, a set of protective habits, and a willingness to disengage the moment you feel manipulated. That way you can still enjoy the incredible craftsmanship and vibrant atmosphere without feeling like a walking wallet.
